## Deliverable: P8 CLOSED-NEGATIVE with a measured mechanism

Built `experiments/analytic/sparse_precision_pi/sparse_precision_pi.py` (19 selftests, all pass), **reusing verbatim** the tested S518 explicit-formula evaluator (so the CLOSED-row-31 branch-cut bug can't recur). It measures the **per-bit cost in the precision domain**: fix x, vary target precision k, count zeros to resolve the first k bits of π(x).

**Verdict — P8's "strictly sub-linear per-bit cost" is REFUTED:**
- **Per-bit cost slope s = 1.5–2.5** (R²≈0.95 at x=10⁵/3·10⁵/10⁶) — each hard bit costs **~3–6× the zeros of the last**: geometric/super-linear, the *opposite* of P8's hope.
- **Mechanism:** error envelope `E ∝ T^{−eta}`, eta≈½ (the L² zeta-zero tail) ⇒ slope `1/eta > 1` (error decays slower than 1/T, so a bit costs more than a doubling of zeros).
- **Free fraction k0/B = 0.88→0.71**, declining with x (R(x) beats its √x worst-case bound at finite x → ~½ only asymptotically = the "~50% of digits", precision-domain).
- **s ≥ 1 is information-forced / kernel-independent** — a sub-linear slope would settle π(x) exactly in sub-√x zeros, contradicting S511/S518. A synthetic `E ∝ T^{−2}` control (slope 0.48) proves the method *would* detect a sub-linear regime if one existed.

**Honest scope:** an adjacent NEGATIVE closure — the precision-domain re-derivation of the SMOOTH+RANDOM info barrier (a known wall from a new angle, *not* novelty; polylog π stays blocked).

